<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sorrento</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Surrentum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sorrento is a town on the Sorrento Peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>. Known in antiquity as Surrentum, it was a self-governing duchy under Byzantine and Neapolitan influence in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> before falling to the Normans in the 12th century. It was the birthplace of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a> and is now a major tourist resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sorrento.jpg" alt="Sorrento" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Sorrento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sorrento is a city and comune on the Sorrentine Peninsula, overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, with views of Vesuvius and the island of Capri. The Amalfi Drive, a narrow road along high cliffs above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>, connects it with Amalfi. In antiquity its position was protected by deep gorges, and only about 300 metres on the south-west needed walls. The line of those walls is followed by the modern town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 8th century BCE the area had a community of indigenous villages that was a crossing point for Etruscan traffic, and it later fell to the Osci, whose ruins at Surrentum date from about 600 BCE. Before coming under the Roman Republic Surrentum was subject to Nuceria. Its temples included those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirens">Sirens</a>. It was known in antiquity for wine, fish and red Campanian vases. An inscription shows that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> restored the town's clock and its decoration in the year after the earthquake of 79 CE.</p><p>Sorrento became an archbishopric around 420. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and then returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Eastern Empire</a>. In the 9th century it became an autonomous duchy, ruled by figures elected by the people who received honorary titles from the Byzantine emperor. It fought Amalfi, the Saracens and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duchies, and with Neapolitan help resisted a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> in 839. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a> acquired the city in 1035, and what remained of its independence ended in 1137, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> conquered it and annexed it to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>On 13 June 1558 the Ottoman navy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Dragut</a> and Piali sacked the town and reportedly took 2,000 captives, which led to a new line of walls. In 1648 there was a revolt against Spanish domination, led by Giovanni Grillo, and in 1861 Sorrento was annexed to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sorrento was the birthplace of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Torquato Tasso</a>, author of the Gerusalemme Liberata. Visitors have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, Keats, Goethe, Ibsen and Nietzsche, who wrote Human, All Too Human there in 1876. The song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a>, with words by Giambattista De Curtis and music by his brother Ernesto, is named for it. Its cathedral dates from the 14th century and has 11th-century doors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. It is a tourist resort, known for ceramics, lacework and marquetry, and it produces limoncello.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sorrento” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Torna a Surriento&quot; (&quot;Come back to <b>Sorrento</b>&quot;) is a Neapolitan song composed by the Italian musician Ernesto De Curtis to words by his brother, the poet and painter Giambattista De Curtis.</blockquote><a href="/songs/torna-a-surriento">Torna a Surriento</a></li><li><blockquote>Naples was a Greek-Latin city of merchants and landowners, governed by dukes who also controlled Cumae, <b>Sorrento</b> and Amalfi for a time.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sorrento</b>, an old Roman town on the peninsula south of the Bay of Naples, was part of the Byzantine Duchy of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sorrento">Duchy of Sorrento</a></li><li><blockquote>Nuceria headed a league of towns in the Sarno valley and along the coast, including Pompeii, Herculaneum, Stabiae and <b>Surrentum</b>, and issued silver coins in Oscan with the name of the Alfaterni.</blockquote><a href="/state/nuceria-alfaterna">Nuceria Alfaterna</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Guaimar IV (1027–1052) the principality reached its height, controlling Amalfi, <b>Sorrento</b> and Capua, and he invested the Norman leaders with Apulia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Principality of Salerno</a></li><li><blockquote>Amalfi was a small fortified town on the steep coast of the <b>Sorrento</b> peninsula, part of the Byzantine Duchy of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Republic of Amalfi</a></li></ul></section>
